==English==

===Alternative forms===
* {{l|en|mouch}}

===Etymology===
From {{etyl|enm|en}} {{term|moochen|lang=enm}}, {{term|mouchen||to pretend poverty|lang=enm}}, from {{etyl|fro|en}} {{term|muchier|lang=fro}}, {{term|mucier|lang=fro}}, {{term|mucer||to skulk, hide, conceal|lang=fro}}, from Old {{etyl|frk|en}} {{recons|mukjan||to hide, conceal oneself|lang=frk}}, from {{etyl|gem-pro|en}} {{recons|mūk-|mukjaną|lang=gem-pro}}, {{recons|mūk-|mūkōną|to hide, ambush|lang=gem-pro}}, from {{etyl|ine-pro|en}} {{recons|(s)mūg-|lang=ine-pro}}, {{recons|(s)mewgʰ-||swindler, thief|lang=ine-pro}}. Cognate with {{etyl|goh|-}} {{term|muhhon|mūhhōn|to store, cache, plunder|lang=goh}}, {{etyl|gmh|-}} {{term|muchen|lang=gmh}}, {{term|mucken||to hide, stash|lang=gmh}}, {{etyl|enm|-}} {{term|muchen|müchen|lang=enm}}, {{term|michen||to rob, steal, pilfer|lang=enm}}. More at {{l|en|mitch}}.

Alternate etymology derives ''mooch'' from {{etyl|enm|en}} {{term|mucchen||to hoard, be stingy|lit=to hide coins in one's nightcap|lang=enm}}, from {{term|mucche||nightcap|lang=enm}}, from {{etyl|dum|en}} {{term|mutse||cap, nightcap|lang=dum}}, from {{etyl|ML.|en}} {{term|almucia||nightcap|lang=la}}, of unknown origin. More at {{l|en|mutch}}, {{l|en|amice}}.

===Pronunciation===
* {{IPA|/muːtʃ/}}
* {{audio|en-us-mooch.ogg|Audio (US)}}
*: {{rhymes|uːtʃ}}

===Verb===
{{en-verb|mooch|es}}

# {{context|British}} To [[wander]] around [[aimlessly]], often causing irritation to others.
# To [[beg]], [[cadge]], or [[sponge]]; to [[exploit]] or [[take advantage]] of others for personal gain.
#* '''1990''', p. 26, Michael L. Frankel & friends, ''Gently with the Tides'', Center for Marine Conservation, Washington (DC), ISBN 1879269-007, p. 26,
#*: I managed to '''mooch''' my way up the journalistic ladder to the next, more impressive level of “Interviewer”.
# {{context|British}} To [[steal]] or [[filch]].
#* '''1922''', [[w:J. S. Fletcher|J. S. Fletcher]], ''The Middle of Things'', ch. 16,
#*: These chaps that '''mooch''' about, as Hyde was doing, pick up all sorts of odds and ends. He may have pinched them from a chemist’s shop.

